# 83. API Traceability

Every consequential action should be traceable:

```text
WHO
 ↓
DID WHAT
 ↓
TO WHICH ENTITY
 ↓
WHEN
 ↓
FROM WHICH STATE
 ↓
TO WHICH STATE
 ↓
WHY
 ↓
SUPPORTED BY WHAT EVIDENCE
```

This is one of AEGIS's most important architectural properties.

---

# 84. API Observability

Every request should be traceable through a correlation identifier.

Conceptually:

```text
Request
 ↓
Correlation ID
 ↓
Application Service
 ↓
Domain Service
 ↓
Database
 ↓
Event
 ↓
Audit
```

This allows engineers to reconstruct failures.

---

# 85. Request Metadata

Where appropriate:

```text
requestId
correlationId
actorId
organizationId
timestamp
clientVersion
```

should be available to the backend.

The server should not blindly trust client-supplied identity metadata.

---

# 86. API Logging

Logs should capture technical information required for debugging without unnecessarily duplicating sensitive operational data.

Do not log sensitive payloads indiscriminately.

Avoid:

```text
console.log(request.body)
```

in production.

---

# 87. API Security Principles

The API must enforce:

```text
authentication
authorization
input validation
rate limiting where appropriate
secure transport
auditability
least privilege
secret management
```

Security architecture will define the complete security model.

---

# 88. Sensitive Information

Sensitive operational or patient-related information must not be returned simply because a database query can retrieve it.

Responses must be scoped to:

```text
purpose
permission
role
organization
context
```

---

# 89. Response Minimization

API responses should return what the client requires.

Avoid returning:

```text
entire patient records
entire evidence stores
entire audit histories
internal infrastructure metadata
```

unless specifically authorized and required.

---

# 90. API Contract Ownership

The API contract is owned by the AEGIS architecture.

Frontend engineers cannot unilaterally change backend contracts.

Backend engineers cannot silently change frontend assumptions.

AI coding agents cannot reinterpret contracts.

Changes require:

```text
proposal
impact analysis
document update
implementation
tests
```

---

# 91. Breaking Change Rule

A change is potentially breaking if it:

```text
removes field
renames field
changes field meaning
changes field type
removes enum value
changes command behavior
changes requiredness
changes authorization semantics
```

Such changes require explicit review.

---

# 92. API Documentation

Every production endpoint should document:

```text
purpose
authentication
authorization
request
response
errors
state requirements
side effects
events emitted
audit behavior
```

---

# 93. Contract Testing

Each API contract should have tests covering:

```text
valid request
invalid request
missing fields
invalid enum
unauthorized actor
invalid state
stale version
duplicate command
not found
successful response
domain event
audit record
```

---

# 94. Integration Testing

Integration tests should verify complete workflows.

Example:

```text
Create anomaly
 ↓
Create threat
 ↓
Create risk assessment
 ↓
Start investigation
 ↓
Add finding
 ↓
Create transmission assessment
 ↓
Propose containment
 ↓
Authorize containment
 ↓
Execute containment
 ↓
Verify containment
 ↓
Start response
 ↓
Complete response
 ↓
Confirm recovery
 ↓
Create preparedness lesson
```

The test must verify both:

```text
state
```

and:

```text
history/events/audit
```

---

# 95. Failure Handling

AEGIS must distinguish:

```text
business rejection
```

from:

```text
technical failure
```

Example:

```text
INVALID_STATE_TRANSITION
```

is not the same as:

```text
DATABASE_UNAVAILABLE
```

The UI must respond differently.

---

# 96. Retry Policy

Automatic retries are appropriate for some transient technical failures.

They are dangerous for some consequential commands.

For example:

```text
GET
```

may safely retry in many cases.

A consequential command requires:

```text
idempotency
```

before automatic retry is considered safe.

---

# 97. Timeout Behavior

Long-running operations must not leave the client guessing.

If an operation exceeds the request timeout:

```text
request timeout
```

does not automatically mean:

```text
operation failed
```

The client should be able to query the resulting entity or operation status.

---

# 98. Long-Running Operations

If an operation cannot complete synchronously:

```text
POST command
 ↓
202 Accepted
 ↓
operation status
 ↓
completion event
```

may be used.

This should only be introduced where necessary.

---

# 99. API Contract for AI Implementation

AI coding agents must treat this document as authoritative.

Before writing API code, the agent must inspect:

```text
01 Master Architecture
02 Domain Model + Global State
03 User Flow + Operational Journey
04 Functional Requirements + 13 Screens
05 Interaction + State Transition
06 Data Architecture + Data Contracts
07 API Architecture + Service Contracts
```

The agent must not implement from a single screen description alone.

---

# 100. AI API Implementation Rules

## Rule 01

Do not invent endpoints.

## Rule 02

Do not invent domain entities.

## Rule 03

Do not invent status values.

## Rule 04

Do not bypass domain services.

## Rule 05

Do not let controllers contain complex domain logic.

## Rule 06

Do not let UI components call persistence directly.

## Rule 07

Do not silently change contracts.

## Rule 08

Do not return database objects blindly.

## Rule 09

Do not trust client authorization claims.

## Rule 10

Do not make consequential commands non-idempotent.

## Rule 11

Do not discard historical state.

## Rule 12

Do not fabricate operational data.

---

# 101. Controller Responsibility

Controllers/routes should handle:

```text
request parsing
authentication context
input validation
calling application services
mapping result to response
error mapping
```

Controllers should not contain:

```text
complex business rules
database orchestration
state-machine logic
risk calculations
authorization shortcuts
```

---

# 102. Application Service Responsibility

Application services coordinate use cases.

Example:

```text
AuthorizeContainment
```

may:

```text
load containment
validate actor
invoke domain rule
persist change
create event
record audit
return result
```

---

# 103. Domain Service Responsibility

Domain services contain domain-specific logic that cannot naturally belong to a single entity.

Examples may include:

```text
ThreatClassificationService
RiskAssessmentService
TransmissionAssessmentService
ContainmentPolicyService
```

The exact services should only be created when domain complexity requires them.

---

# 104. Repository Responsibility

Repositories provide persistence access.

They should answer:

```text
save
find
update
query
```

They should not determine whether:

```text
containment is authorized
```

That belongs to domain/application logic.

---

# 105. Dependency Direction

The architecture should maintain:

```text
API
 ↓
Application
 ↓
Domain
 ↓
Infrastructure
```

not:

```text
Domain
 ↓
UI
```

and not:

```text
Database
 ↓
UI
```

---

# 106. Service Dependency Rule

Avoid circular dependencies.

Bad:

```text
ThreatService
 ↓
RiskService
 ↓
ThreatService
```

Prefer explicit orchestration through an application workflow or appropriate domain abstraction.

---

